.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1232 PM PDT Tue Sep 22 2026
(This evening through Wednesday night)

An upper level trough is moving across Nevada this afternoon and
will continue to shift into the Great Basin on Wednesday. As such,
temperatures this afternoon are forecast to warm into the 60s near
the coast, upper 60s to low 70s around the bayshore, and mid 70s to
mid 80s across the interior with mostly sunny sky conditions across
the interior. Breezy onshore flow will develop in the afternoon and
evening hours with gusts of 20 to 30 mph near the coast, in the
favored gaps and passes, and down the Salinas Valley.

As the marine layer gradually compresses to around 1,500 feet
tonight, we are expecting less of an inland intrusion of stratus. As
such, temperatures are expected to cool into the 50s for much of the
region overnight. More of a zonal flow is forecast on Wednesday
which will lead to a warming trend. Temperatures Wednesday afternoon
are forecast to reach into the upper 60s to mid 70s near the coast,
upper 70s to mid 80s around the bayshore, and mid 80s to low 90s
across the interior. The warmest interior portions of the Central
Coast look to warm into low-to-upper 90s. This will result in
pockets of moderate HeatRisk across the interior. As a result, drier
conditions are forecast in elevations above the marine influences.

Tomorrow night, look for again less inland extent of stratus and the
warming trend to continue. Overnight temperatures are forecast to be
in the mid 50s near the coast to lower 60s across the interior.
However, the warming air mass aloft will result in the higher
terrain only cooling into the 70s.

